#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5444" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and $T$ is invertible?\n\n1. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n2. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n3. $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3$, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $Im(T) = R^{3}; rank(T) = 3; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" :question/math-academy-id "e-9985" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{__________}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n2. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n3. $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3$, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $Im(T) = R^{3}; rank(T) = 3; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3$. Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3$, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124354 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9986" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $W W = R^{n}; rank(T) = n; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n> $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$ $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following:\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $Im (T) = R^{n}; rank(T) = n; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n> $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$ $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ad56bf-2ae2-58d1-a6f6-116b6ff257bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bijection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121280} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bcf6d8-066f-5ac1-afb8-ea7b0beea899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121281} {:answer/id #uuid "fe17de18-2f2e-5771-bb5e-ebe76c364eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not bijection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121282} {:answer/id #uuid "58a2ee5b-fe17-5df4-92dd-990c0840ca3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not injection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121283} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc0d546-a802-52c7-8bc4-d9c1af6bbe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "injection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ad56bf-2ae2-58d1-a6f6-116b6ff257bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bijection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121280} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3edd9e9-3e9b-5cb2-9b70-d6aabbec58ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121279}] :question/id #uuid "9859f901-d01e-5f5b-a45d-d37e251b90a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141449" :question/problem "> $T$ is a $W W W$ $⟺{Im(T) = R^{n}; rank(T) = n; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n> $⟺$ $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T: R^{n} → R^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5df8913-3a20-56ef-89a7-01dbbea7c04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123383} {:answer/id #uuid "c00cf7cc-2217-517c-bcaf-c7bad9f5fdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123384} {:answer/id #uuid "ea52efd8-617c-5537-a80a-f508dca62b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123385} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa768ea-d670-5ccf-9262-9dd13f2fcbeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123386} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd04760-c131-518f-a299-746b83a43031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aa768ea-d670-5ccf-9262-9dd13f2fcbeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123386} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce41e18-2000-5596-b2f9-936d3e8e9258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123382}] :question/id #uuid "3350b458-d932-58ce-9841-3d6f73360368" :question/math-academy-id "q-38977" :question/problem "Let $T: R^{4} → R^{4}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $T$ is invertible, then $__________$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $Ker(T) = {0}$\n2. $rank(T) \\ne 4$\n3. $nullity(T) \\ne 0$"}]}
